+# Fix for: Color mapping fails with boolean data
+
+## Issue
+When using boolean values for the `color` parameter in `seaborn.objects.Plot`, a TypeError occurs:
+```
+TypeError: numpy boolean subtract, the `-` operator, is not supported, use the bitwise_xor, the `^` operator, or the logical_xor function instead.
+```
+
+Example failing code:
+```python
+so.Plot(["a", "b"], [1, 2], color=[True, False]).add(so.Bar())
+```
+
+## Root Cause
+In `seaborn/_core/scales.py`, the `ContinuousBase._setup()` method attempts to normalize continuous data by computing:
+```python
+b = forward(vmax) - forward(vmin)
+```
+
+When `vmin` and `vmax` are boolean values (which happens when boolean data is treated as numeric), this operation fails because NumPy doesn't support subtraction on boolean arrays.
+
+## Solution
+Convert boolean values to float before performing arithmetic operations in the normalization setup:
+
+```python
+# Convert boolean values to float to allow arithmetic operations
+# numpy.bool_ subtraction is not supported
+if isinstance(vmin, (bool, np.bool_)):
+    vmin = float(vmin)
+if isinstance(vmax, (bool, np.bool_)):
+    vmax = float(vmax)
+```
+
+This fix handles both:
+- Python's built-in `bool` type
+- NumPy's `np.bool_` type
+
+## Location
+File: `seaborn/_core/scales.py`
+Method: `ContinuousBase._setup()`
+Lines: 350-355 (after `axis.convert_units()` call)
+
+## Effect
+- Boolean values are properly converted to float (True → 1.0, False → 0.0)
+- The subtraction operation works correctly
+- The normalize function is created successfully
+- Boolean data can now be mapped to colors in plots
